
  The next question is the one that determines the size of everything downstream.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether "users" is plural. Both readings are consistent with what you asked for, and they produce systems
  that share almost no machinery.

  - [1mMulti-user collaboration[0m — "tasks they care about" means tasks [3mother people[0m touched. Buys you the thing you
    described literally. Costs: identity, auth, a server, permissions, subscription records, fan-out on every mutation,
    and per-user delivery preferences. Rules out staying a static page — you need a backend. Expensive to add later,
    cheap to scope down from.
  - [1mSingle-user reminders[0m — "changes" means due dates arriving, recurring tasks firing, things going stale. Buys you a
    useful feature for a fraction of the work: a scheduler, a permission prompt, local storage. No identity, no fan-out,
    no server. Rules out collaboration later without a rewrite of the storage layer.
  - [1mSingle-user now, multi-user shaped[0m — build local and single-user, but chokepoint mutations through an event log and
    treat "actor" as a field from day one. Buys optionality. Costs some ceremony that looks pointless while there's one
    user.

  [1mMy recommendation: the third[0m, unless you already know collaboration is coming. The empty repo says this is early, and
  the expensive mistake at this stage isn't picking the smaller system — it's scattering task mutations across the
  codebase so that adding notifications later means auditing every write. The event-log discipline is cheap now and is
  the one thing that's genuinely painful to retrofit.
